    "explanation": "To the driver, Mr D-28347: your claim to keep the 1.47 SGD is not supported, and the refund already credited to the rider stands. To the rider, R-59266: the 1.47 SGD you were refunded stays with you. Nothing further is refunded or recovered from either of you.\n\nThe route. The recommended route was 12.4 km (trip_record.optimal_distance_km) and the route actually driven was 13.8 km (trip_record.actual_distance_km), so the deviation is 1.40 km under POL-RD-22 — over the 1.0 km tolerance. The excess distance fare is the deviation distance times the per-km rate, without surge: 1.40 km x 1.05 SGD/km = 1.47 SGD (POL-RD-24). That is exactly the 1.47 SGD the fare ledger records as already refunded to the rider and recovered from your earnings (refund_issued_sgd 1.47; payment_status PARTIALLY_REFUNDED).\n\nThe notice. Row (a) of POL-RD-24 — the only row that would leave the fare with you — needs the rider to have been 'informed and agreed'. POL-RD-21 requires you to inform the rider before or at the point of deviation. The platform's own record puts the deviation at 19:21:30 (route_deviation_detected at the CTE / Balestier Road exit; the GPS trace is on Thomson Road from 19:21:11), while your only message about it, MSG-06005-003, is timestamped 19:24:10 — about two minutes forty seconds later, and written in the past tense ('CTE got breakdown, I already come out at Thomson'). Your account that you told the passenger before you left the CTE is contradicted by the chat log's own timestamps, and no earlier verbal notice is in the record (the trip's in_app_calls list is empty, and POL-RD-21 says a verbal notice with no recording cannot be established). The rider's 'Ok' at 19:24:50 came after the reroute had begun and does not cure the late notice. The rider was therefore not informed as the clause requires, so row (b)/(d) governs and the excess distance fare returns to the rider.\n\nThe traffic question (raised by both sides) does not decide this case: the excess distance fare is refunded whether the reroute was traffic-justified (row b) or not (row d), so the LTA vehicle-breakdown advisory on the CTE does not change the money. No fact the ruling needs is left unestablished, so POL-GEN-26 does not call for escalation.\n\nWho decides. The amount at stake is 1.47 SGD, below the 10.00 SGD band, so under POL-GEN-22 the AI ruling is final; neither profile carries a fraud flag; there is no safety indicator, so POL-GEN-21 does not apply; and the deviation is under 2.0 km. POL-RD-25 sets the required confidence for a route deviation at 0.80, and this ruling meets it. Because you filed, the outcome is read against the present state: your claim fails and the present state stands — NO_ACTION, with both amounts null."
