    "explanation": "Both sides agree on the shape of this trip, and the money turns on one question: was the rider told about the reroute before or at the point of deviation, as POL-RD-21 requires?\n\nTHE DEVIATION AND THE MONEY. The platform recorded the vehicle leaving the recommended route (CTE / Balestier Road exit) at 19:21:30 and rejoining at Ang Mo Kio Avenue 6 at 19:38:23. On POL-RD-22's recorded distances the deviation is 13.8 km minus 12.4 km = 1.40 km, above the 1.0 km tolerance, so the forgiving rows do not apply. Under POL-RD-24 the excess distance fare is 1.40 km x 1.05 SGD/km = 1.47 SGD, without surge. That is exactly the 1.47 SGD the ledger shows already refunded to the rider and recovered from the driver (fare_adjusted at 19:50:11; refund_issued_sgd 1.47; payment_status PARTIALLY_REFUNDED).\n\nTRAFFIC JUSTIFICATION (driver's claim, accepted). The LTA advisory EV-ADVIS-06005-01 reports a VEHICLE_BREAKDOWN on the CTE towards SLE after the Braddell Road exit, an admissible 'incident' type under POL-RD-23, on the expressway the recommended route uses and in the direction of travel, valid 18:54-20:02, overlapping the trip window. The reroute was traffic-justified.\n\nNOTIFICATION (rider's claims, accepted; driver's claim rejected). POL-RD-21 requires the driver to inform the rider 'before or at the point of deviation.' The platform put the deviation at 19:21:30. The driver's first and only message about it, MSG-06005-003, was sent at 19:24:10 and speaks in the past tense ('I already come out at Thomson') - roughly 2 minutes 40 seconds after the deviation began. The driver's filing says he told the passenger 'before I left the CTE'; the chat log's own timestamps contradict that. No earlier verbal notice can be shown: the trip record's in_app_calls is empty, and POL-RD-21 provides that a verbal notice with no recording in the record cannot be established. The rider's 'Ok' at 19:24:50 came after the reroute was already done; it acknowledges being told of a fait accompli, not agreement to a choice he could exercise, and so does not make the deviation 'informed and agreed.' Row (a) of POL-RD-24 therefore does not apply. The case falls in row (b) (traffic-justified, rider not informed, deviation over 1 km) - and, on the rider's argument that the advisory is not affirmatively tied to the route, row (d) gives the same figure - so the 1.47 SGD excess distance fare is refundable to the rider.\n\nOUTCOME. Because the driver filed this dispute, the ruling is read from the driver's claim against the present state (POL-GEN-03). The rider is owed the 1.47 SGD already refunded; the driver's claim for the money back is not supported; the present state stands. NO_ACTION, both money fields null. This is not the residual case POL-RD-24 sends to escalation: the rider was informed (late) and answered 'Ok,' he did not object, so the case fits a row.\n\nCONFIDENCE. The artifacts carrying the deciding facts - the trip record's deviation event and the chat log's timestamps - are both COMPLETE at weight_multiplier 1.0, so POL-GEN-02 caps nothing below 1.0. The contested value is 1.47 SGD, below the 10.00 SGD band, so POL-GEN-22 makes the AI ruling final, and POL-RD-25's 0.80 confidence floor for a route deviation is met. Neither profile carries a fraud flag; no safety indicator appears, so POL-GEN-21 has no work to do. No route-deviation referral clause applies in this version.",
